over the decades of television and film  depictions of the riddler several  talented actors have played the  over-the-top villain but which version  of the riddler truly captures the  essence of the character keep watching  to see every live-action version of the  riddler ranked worst to best  better known for his portrayal of gomez  adams on the original adams family tv  series john aston also showed up on  another iconic 60s genre show in 1967 he  was cast as the riddler on the second  season of abc's classic adam west  starring batman series subbing in for  the role's original performer frank  gorshian  to this day there are different  explanations floating around as to why  gorshin was replaced either he was busy  with a gig he couldn't get out of or he  demanded a pay raise that producers  refused to give him whatever the truth  of the matter the result was the same  aston while a great actor was pointedly  a replacement and he felt like one but  i've only started with the cape  crusaders i'm going to confound them  with conundrums unnerve them with  enigmas perplexed them with puzzles  until they wish they were dead  although he did a fine job with the role  aston simply didn't have enough time to  really make the character his own under  the shadow of gorshin's work indeed by  the next season gorshin had already been  brought back as if nothing had happened  still aston did manage to give his  portrayal some memorable touches such as  a cane twirl that would later prove an  influence on jim carrey's tank  batman forever is not a lot of people's  favorite cinematic iteration of the  caped crusaders mythos but decades later  this take on batman through the lens of  nostalgia has aged nicely for some fans  and one of the many imperfect elements  that some fans have a soft spot for is  jim carrey's performance as the riddler  heavily influenced by frank gorshian's  classic turn this was the first  live-action version of the character to  get a proper backstory and the first to  be referred to by his common name edward  nigma on paper it seemed like the  perfect role for carrie with a descent  into madness arc that could play to his  strengths as both a comedic and dramatic  performer but the broad ham-fisted  writing didn't tamp fully into the  things that made the riddler complex and  interesting as a villain elements like  his hubris his deep insecurity masked by  grandstanding and his tremendous  intelligence were generally left by the  wayside who are you  just a friend  but you can call me  the riddler  kerry was therefore stranded on an  island of goofiness left to carry the  character with just his sheer madcap  energy and commitment the performance is  highly charismatic and memorable it is  jim carrey after all but the film's take  on the character is subpar  there's no doubt that inconsistent  writing on gotham led to a few rough  patches in terms of character work over  the years but the fox prequel series  deserves kudos for deepening and  enriching the backstories of several  batman villains and that was especially  true of corey michael smith's edward  nigma this version of enigma was  different from previous riddlers in that  the villain was presented as a fully  rounded three-dimensional character with  an engrossing storyline all his own  gotham introduced him as an eccentric  ostracized forensic scientist at the  gotham city police department as he  began to realize that his intelligence  gave him an upper hand over the people  who mistreated and belittled him we got  to see him slowly give in to his  narcissistic psychopathic impulses and  assume the persona of a full-fledged  villain you want to know why i could  never love someone like you oh i'm  warning you because you are a spoiled  child who throws a tantrum anytime he  doesn't get what he wants  smith was an absolute superstar in the  role allowing us to empathize with be  terrified of or absolutely loathe the  living daylights out of nigma many  batman fans would argue that it was the  best ever translation of the spirit of  the comics riddler to the screen  if corey michael smith's edward nigma  stands tall as arguably the most perfect  orthodox take on the riddler paul dano's  edward nashton is making a splash for  the exact opposite reason the savvy  retrofitting of the riddler into the  newest batman film is a terrifying  serial killer and maybe the most out  there live-action interpretation of the  character yet but he's so effective so  indelible that we wouldn't be surprised  if dana's performance ended up  influencing future riddlers for decades  to  [Music]  come what makes nashton such a brilliant  villain is that writers matt reeves and  peter craig were able to hone in on the  most essential psychological aspects of  the riddler the writers placed those  elements within a context that wholly  reshapes who the riddler is the core  theme of the character has always been  power its pursuit its exchange the way  it can shift at the drop of a hat on the  most basic level the riddler is a man  who renders his enemies helpless the  batman understands that disposition for  the perfect horror movie fodder it is  reeves film rings unspeakable dread out  of nashton's way of messing with batman  gotham city pd and his victims almost  like a cat playing with a mouse  the way the character is integrated into  real world social dynamics is similarly  pitch perfect his hordes of rabid social  media followers mirroring the sense of  bottomless despair that today is  extremist cult mentality prone internet  ecosystem tends to invite it's not the  riddler we're used to but it's the  riddler for our times  as the decades have brought about darker  deeper emotionally heftier screen  adaptations of the batman comics the  majority of the villains on the  light-hearted 60s batman series have  been surpassed by subsequent versions  that however is not the case with frank  gorshian's riddler the actor's take on  the character essentially define the  riddler as we know him from personality  to mannerisms to that unmistakable evil  flair  i am the prince of puzzlers the count of  conundrums the king of crime i hold  court here no one else  before the classic batman show the  riddler had been mostly relegated to  minor one-off appearances in the comics  it was the runaway success of the  character's tv appearances that inspired  dc comics to feature him more and more  prominently they eventually turned him  into a fixture of the batman rogues  gallery and the inevitable influence of  gorshin's performance is the way  audiences came to know him it wouldn't  be much of an exaggeration to say that  gorshin is the riddler the success of  gorshin's work earned him a prime time  emmy nod for outstanding performance by  an actor and a supporting role in a  comedy that led to subsequent  appearances in the 1966 film spin-off  and the 1979 tv special legends of the  superheroes  every riddler sense whether imitating it  deconstructing it or subverting it has  been a response to gorham's riddler on  some level check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about the batman are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

